/**
 * ESTRIX BLOCKS — every piece in the game: regular blocks and powerups.
 * Edit by hand or with block_designer.html (it keeps this comment when saving).
 *
 * Each entry:
 * {
 *   id: "unique_id",          lowercase letters, numbers and _
 *   name: "shown in game",
 *   grid: ["XX", "X."],       the shape: X = block, . = empty
 *   color: "#hex" | [[...]],  a solid colour, or pixel art: a grid of hex colours exactly
 *                             k times the size of `grid` (k = pixels per block, 1-9).
 *                             "" pixels inside a block show the first colour in the grid.
 *   difficulty: 0-6,          regular blocks only; drives when it is served
 *                             (see CONFIG.assist.pieceMix in config.js). 6 = level 6: only
 *                             ever comes in its group (shapes/groups.js), once unlocked
 *   frequency: 0-1,           how often it is picked compared with others of its kind
 *                             (0 = retired: never served, but kept in the file)
 *   rotation: { mode: "any" | "none" },
 *
 *   Optional:
 *   nextShapes: ["id"],       blocks that may come straight after this one ("back to back")...
 *   nextShapeProbs: [0.5],    ...with these probabilities
 *   seam: "iceberg",          pieces drawn to join up into one picture (the tip and the rest of
 *                             the iceberg) share a seam name: no edge is drawn where they touch
 *   nameAgain: "another raindrop",  the name it goes by when it comes straight after itself
 *   echo: "other_id",         in a level 6 set: that piece comes back later in the same run
 *   noShake: true,            big pieces (difficulty 4-6) shake the board when they land; this
 *                             one doesn't (e.g. a swarm of bees)
 *   shake: true,              this one does, however small (e.g. a small boulder)
 *   once: true,               it only ever turns up once a game (turbo zen: once since the page
 *                             was loaded), e.g. a sock with no match
 *   oops: { many: "garden gnomes", short: "gnomes" },
 *                             turbo zen, a board full of it: its plural ("Oops, all garden
 *                             gnomes!", "the strategic reserve of garden gnomes") and a short form
 *                             for the names that say it over and over ("gnomes gnomes gnomes!!").
 *                             Left out, they're worked out from its name.
 *   remix: { mix: ["townhouses", "schnauzers"] } | { scramble: "spam" },
 *                             rebuilt at random every time it arrives: `mix` blends two pieces
 *                             drawn on the same grid (and the same k), block by block; `scramble`
 *                             shuffles another piece's blocks into this piece's shape. Its own
 *                             grid and colours are what the designer shows.
 *   tags: ["food"],           labels used by achievements (shapes/achievements.js), e.g.
 *                             "food", "medicine", "squelchy"
 *   zones: {                  named regions around the piece, in the same format as powerup
 *     inside: { origin, grid } areas (below). Used by achievements, e.g. the inside of a mouth.
 *   },
 *   powerup: { ... },         makes this block a powerup (Normal mode only), see below
 *   claudeDraft: true,        made by Claude and not yet reviewed by a human. The block
 *                             designer shows these with a badge and removes the flag as soon
 *                             as you edit and save the piece. The game ignores it.
 * }
 *
 * powerup: {
 *   type: "destroyer" | "gravity" | "expander" | "acid" | "blast" | "goo" | "phantom" | "combo",
 *     destroyer: destroys every block in its area (its own blocks are always in it, however
 *                it's turned, whatever the area as drawn: it goes too)
 *     gravity:   blocks in its area fall (or slide) until they hit something, the piece's own
 *                blocks too (it stays, as an expander does), each into the gaps under it. It's
 *                always in its own area, however it's turned, and so is everything under it
 *                (the game adds them, whatever the area as drawn)
 *     expander:  fills every empty cell in its area with `fill`
 *     acid:      dissolves every block it touches (its area is worked out from its shape:
 *                every cell within `reach` steps of one of its blocks, plus any `area`, and
 *                its own blocks, as a destroyer's)
 *     blast:     throws the blocks in its area outwards by up to `push` cells, then they fall
 *     goo:       melts: its blocks (× `volume`) flow down into the lowest gaps they can reach
 *     phantom:   falls straight through blocks into the deepest gap it fits; stays as blocks
 *     combo:     several powerups in one: `parts` (see below), mid-to-late game only
 *   reach: 1,                 acid only: how far it dissolves (steps from its blocks, default 1)
 *   push: 3,                  blast only: how far blocks get thrown (cells, default 3)
 *   volume: 1.5,              goo only: how many cells it fills, × its own block count (default 1)
 *   intro: "...",             optional: a line or two for the powerup's intro screen
 *   starter: true,            optional: a good, simple first example of its class. The first
 *                             piece of each class in a game is always a starter (if one is usable).
 *   help: 1-5,                how helpful it is (1 = barely, 5 = a lifesaver). The more the
 *                             player is struggling, the more the helpful ones are picked
 *                             (CONFIG.assist.powerups.struggle). Default from tier.
 *   parts: [ {...}, {...} ],  combo only: each part is a mini powerup with its own type
 *                             ("destroyer", "acid", "blast", "gravity" or "expander") and that
 *                             type's fields (area, direction, reach, push, fill). They fire in
 *                             that order (destroy first, fill last). A combo only turns up once
 *                             all of its parts' classes are in the game.
 *   direction: "down" | "left" | "right" | "up",   gravity only (default "down")
 *   tier: 1 | 2 | 3,          1 = gentle ... 3 = very strong. Stronger tiers are served more
 *                             when the stack is high (CONFIG.assist.powerups in config.js)
 *   description: "...",       optional: replaces the effect text shown under the name
 *   area: {                   the highlighted ("affected") cells:
 *     origin: [x, y],         where the piece's top-left block sits inside `grid`
 *     grid: ["..."],          "." not affected, "#" this cell, "-" this cell's whole row,
 *   },                        "|" this cell's whole column, "+" both row and column,
 *                             "v" this cell and every cell under it, down to the floor
 *   fill: "#hex" | [[...]],   expander / goo: art for the cells it fills (one block of k×k pixels)
 *     | { picture: [[...]], k: 6, slice: { top: 1, bottom: 1, left: 0, right: 0 } }
 *                             expander (or a combo's expander part): one picture drawn over the
 *                             whole of the area instead (k pixels per block, a whole number of
 *                             blocks each way), laid over the box the area spans from where the
 *                             piece is (its "v" and "|" columns reaching the floor). The `slice`
 *                             blocks at each edge stay as drawn; the rest repeats to fit the box
 *                             (or is cut down the middle when the box is smaller), so a waterfall
 *                             keeps its top and its splash however far it falls
 *     | { gen: "noise", k: 6, palette: ["#hex", ...], ... }   (or layers: [{ gen, ... }, ...])
 *                             expander (or a combo's expander part): ART PAINTED TO FIT what it
 *                             actually fills, at any size, the moment it fills it, a little
 *                             different every time. It knows where each pixel is on the board,
 *                             how far it is from the piece, and how far from the edge of the fill,
 *                             so lava can glow by the vent and crust at its edges, rays can fan
 *                             out from a sun, a marshmallow can be one round shaded blob. Painters:
 *                             solid, noise, gradient, strata, streaks, rays, rings, blob (sphere |
 *                             pillow), bubbles, tiles (running | stack | checker | basket | hex |
 *                             scales | diamond), texture (a hand-drawn pattern spanning several
 *                             cells that repeats across the board), picture (as above). Around them:
 *                             shade (a bevel round the whole fill), edge (an outline), glow (near
 *                             the piece), depth (darker further down), grain, vary (colours shifted
 *                             at random each time), palettes (colourways, one picked each time),
 *                             cover: true (the piece's own cells are part of it: repainted, or
 *                             filled in where it vanished), join: true (no edge between the piece
 *                             and its fill). Every setting is described at the top of src/fillart.js.
 *   consume: true,            optional: vanish when fired (default: yes, except expanders and
 *                             gravity, whose blocks stay; a combo, if one of its parts would)
 *   collapse: false,          destroyer / acid: wiped wall-to-wall rows close up like a line clear
 *   scatter: { radius: 6, density: 0.6 },  destroyer only: instead of `area`, a random spray of
 *                             cells around the piece (thinning out from the centre, in streaks
 *                             and clumps), rolled afresh each time the piece turns up
 *   bad: true,                optional: a powerup that mostly makes things worse (the
 *                             existential ones). Never early, never while its class is new
 *                             (CONFIG.assist.powerups.bad). Keep these rare and help 1.
 * }
 *
 * For powerups, `frequency` is compared with the other powerups only. Powerups turn like any
 * other piece, but only the piece turns: the highlighted area never rotates (it stays as drawn,
 * centred on the piece).
 */

